-- Agency.hp — what holds of any agent over this theory, whatever it believes.
--
-- THE ENGINE ANSWERS ABOUT ONE AGENT'S FACTS; these theorems hold of every agent at once. That is
-- the division of labour between the two dialects, and in a BDI system it matters more than
-- anywhere else: an agent's beliefs change constantly, and what is true OF believing does not.
open Agency
axioms [serialB, serialD, serialI, transB, euclidB, realism]

-- ── belief's introspection ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- AN AGENT KNOWS WHAT IT BELIEVES — axiom 4, and the first half of why belief is KD45.
theorem belief_composes : ∀ i w u v, believes(i, w, u) ⇒ believes(i, u, v) ⇒ believes(i, w, v)
proof
  intro i w u v first second
  apply transB [first, second]
qed

-- AND KNOWS WHAT IT DOES NOT BELIEVE — axiom 5, the second half.
theorem belief_is_euclidean : ∀ i w u v, believes(i, w, u) ⇒ believes(i, w, v) ⇒ believes(i, u, v)
proof
  intro i w u v first second
  apply euclidB [first, second]
qed

-- INTROSPECTION COMPOSES WITH ITSELF. Nothing here is available for desire or intention, which is
-- the whole content of "belief is KD45 and the other two are KD".
theorem belief_reaches_three_deep : ∀ i w u v x, believes(i, w, u) ⇒ believes(i, u, v) ⇒ believes(i, v, x) ⇒ believes(i, w, x)
proof
  intro i w u v x first second third
  have onward : believes(i, u, x) by apply transB [second, third]
  apply transB [first, onward]
qed

-- ── realism, and what it gives ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- WHAT AN AGENT DESIRES, IT INTENDS. Read back through the modal translation this is `I(i) p ⇒
-- D(i) p`: the desire relation is contained in the intention relation, so anything true throughout
-- the intended worlds is true throughout the desired ones. The inclusion is the frame condition;
-- the implication between modalities is what it MEANS.
theorem what_is_desired_is_intended : ∀ i w u, desires(i, w, u) ⇒ intends(i, w, u)
proof
  intro i w u wanted
  apply realism [wanted]
qed

-- A SEEDED DESIRE IS THEREFORE AN INTENTION, in two steps: seriality makes the seed a desire, and
-- realism carries it across. This is the shape every "an agent that wants X will try for X" claim
-- reduces to, and it is worth seeing it reduce.
theorem a_seeded_desire_is_intended : ∀ i w u, desireSucc(i, w, u) ⇒ intends(i, w, u)
proof
  intro i w u seeded
  have wanted : desires(i, w, u) by apply serialD [seeded]
  apply realism [wanted]
qed

-- ── seriality, and what it does NOT say ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- THIS IS THE WHOLE OF AXIOM D AS THIS THEORY CAN STATE IT: a seeded successor IS an accessible
-- world. What it does not say -- and what no theorem here can say -- is that every world HAS one.
-- That is a precondition on the runtime, and `Agency.hs` says so in those words.
theorem a_seeded_belief_is_accessible : ∀ i w u, beliefSucc(i, w, u) ⇒ believes(i, w, u)
proof
  intro i w u seeded
  apply serialB [seeded]
qed

theorem an_intention_seeded_is_held : ∀ i w u, intentionSucc(i, w, u) ⇒ intends(i, w, u)
proof
  intro i w u seeded
  apply serialI [seeded]
qed
